  {"id":130197,"slug":"ulva-intestinalis","sci":"Ulva intestinalis","common":"Gutweed","family":"Ulvaceae","obs":955,"regions":["olympics"],"months":[2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":13755949,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Marissa Murphy","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":405970042,"ext":"jpeg","by":"CJ Campbell","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":37793912,"ext":"jpeg","by":"ðejay (Orkney)","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":318070069,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Денис Кокшаров","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":10018215,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Brett Sandford","lic":"CC BY"}],"wiki":"Ulva intestinalis","summary":"Ulva intestinalis, formerly referred to as Enteromorpha intestinalis (Linnaeus) Nees, is a green alga in the division Chlorophyta, of the genus Ulva (sea lettuce), also known by the common names gutweed and grass kelp. Until they were reclassified by genetic work completed in the early 2000s, the tubular members of the genus Ulva were in the genus Enteromorpha."},
